---
import RecordUpdate from "../../components/RecordUpdate.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
import ArticleSummary from "../../components/ArticleSummary.astro";
import ArticleMetadata from "../../components/ArticleMetadata.astro";
import WhyThisMatters from "../../components/WhyThisMatters.astro";
import WhatWeKnow from "../../components/WhatWeKnow.astro";
import WhatWeDontKnow from "../../components/WhatWeDontKnow.astro";
import QuestionsResidentsShouldAsk from "../../components/QuestionsResidentsShouldAsk.astro";
import AccountabilityTracker from "../../components/AccountabilityTracker.astro";
import RelatedCoverage from "../../components/RelatedCoverage.astro";

const title = "Budget & Spending Watch | StayJustIn";
const description = "A living tracker for West Covina budget and spending questions, including sewer reserves, capital planning, budget amendments, grants, contracts, emergency spending, and city funds.";
---

<MainLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical="https://www.stayjustin.com/accountability/budget-spending/"
>
  <section class="article-page">
    <div class="container article-container">
      <p class="page-kicker">Accountability Hub</p>

      <h1>Budget & Spending Watch</h1>

      <RecordUpdate title="October records add funding holds and new spending questions" paragraphs={["The October 20 CIP reports Fire Station 1 replacement design on hold pending funds and Citrus/Cameron construction awaiting funding. New agenda requests include a $50,000 Altamira preliminary study and purchases for two grant-funded playground projects.", "The fire-dispatch report’s long numeric string is an account code, not a $110,502.32 budget cap. The proposed agreement also has extraordinary-cost exceptions to its ordinary annual rate limit. Vote outcomes and executed agreements still need verification.", "The sewer questions below are retained from earlier reporting. This update does not establish that the City has or has not answered them since the original entry."]} href="/city-hall/october-20-2026-agenda-report/" />

      <ArticleMetadata
        category="Budget / Public Spending"
        updated="November 11, 2026 | Adopted budget analysis added"
      />

      <section class="adopted-budget" aria-labelledby="adopted-budget-heading">
        <p class="explainer-kicker">New document review · November 11, 2026</p>
        <h2 id="adopted-budget-heading">West Covina's adopted $148.4 million budget: what residents should know</h2>
        <p>On June 16, 2026, the City Council unanimously adopted Resolution No. 2026-44 for fiscal year July 1, 2026 through June 30, 2027. The figures below are <strong>budget estimates, not confirmed actual spending</strong>. Source: Resolution No. 2026-44, Exhibit A (pages 4–7) and Exhibit B (pages 8–12).</p>
        <div class="budget-stats">
          <div><span>Expected citywide revenue</span><strong>$162.04 million</strong></div>
          <div><span>Budgeted citywide expenditures</span><strong>$148.43 million</strong></div>
          <div><span>General Fund projected annual gain</span><strong>$107,896</strong></div>
          <div><span>General Fund projected ending balance</span><strong>$26.07 million</strong></div>
        </div>
        <h3>Where the $148.43 million is budgeted</h3>
        <p>These are accounting fund groups, not a department-by-department breakdown. Transfers are separately recorded.</p>
        <div class="budget-bars" role="img" aria-label="Budgeted expenditures: General Fund 85.76 million dollars, special revenue 30.21 million, debt service 16.05 million, internal services 12.41 million, capital project funds 2.64 million, other funds 1.35 million.">
          {[
            ["General Fund", 85.76],
            ["Special revenue", 30.21],
            ["Debt service", 16.05],
            ["Internal services", 12.41],
            ["Capital project funds", 2.64],
            ["Other funds", 1.35]
          ].map(([name, amount]) => (
            <div class="budget-bar-row">
              <span>{name}</span>
              <div class="budget-bar-track"><div class="budget-bar-fill" style={`width: ${Number(amount) / 85.76 * 100}%`}></div></div>
              <strong>${Number(amount).toFixed(2)}M</strong>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
        <h3>What it means for residents</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>A tight General Fund plan:</strong> The city expects to add only $107,896 after operating expenditures and net transfers, despite projecting a $26.07 million ending fund balance. That is a narrow annual margin, not an assertion that reserves are exhausted.</li>
          <li><strong>Insurance obligations:</strong> General liability and workers' compensation funds project negative ending balances of about $1.63 million and $3.66 million respectively. Ask for audited balances, claims forecasts, and a funding plan.</li>
          <li><strong>Staffing and overtime:</strong> Authorized full-time Police positions decline from 160 to 156, and Fire positions from 75 to 74. Authorized positions do not show actual vacancies or daily coverage; the city's overtime savings assumptions need payroll and vacancy data.</li>
          <li><strong>Code enforcement:</strong> Authorized full-time Code Enforcement Officer positions increase from four to nine. Residents deserve an explanation of cost, priorities, and performance measures.</li>
          <li><strong>Capital projects:</strong> Resolution Section 5 automatically carries forward unspent approved capital appropriations. The earlier $48.46 million project listing cannot be directly compared with the $2.64 million annual capital-project-fund expenditure category; other funds also finance capital work. A project-by-project reconciliation is still needed.</li>
        </ul>
        <h3>Ethics and accountability</h3>
        <p>The budget does not itself demonstrate misconduct. The public-interest questions are whether priorities are fair, future obligations are disclosed, project spending can be traced to results, and authorized adjustments are transparent. Sections 3–4 permit specified administrative reallocations and certain grant or donation appropriations up to $60,000 per source under stated conditions.</p>
        <h3>Questions residents should ask</h3>
        <ol>
          <li>How much of the projected overtime reduction has been achieved, and are response times changing?</li>
          <li>What is the plan for negative self-insurance fund balances?</li>
          <li>Which carried-forward capital projects are delayed, and what has actually been spent?</li>
          <li>What services and metrics justify expanded code enforcement staffing?</li>
          <li>Where are quarterly budget-to-actual reports and subsequent budget amendments published?</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="source-note"><strong>Primary record:</strong> City of West Covina, Resolution No. 2026-44, adopted June 16, 2026, Attachment No. 1, Exhibits A and B. The resolution's 12-page attachment contains summary schedules, not the complete detailed project ledger. <strong>Open verification:</strong> final detailed adopted budget, quarterly actuals, capital-project balances, and staffing vacancies.</p>
      </section>

      <ArticleSummary text="StayJustIn's Budget & Spending Watch tracks major West Covina funds, reserves, contracts, grants, emergency spending, assessment districts, and the plain-English questions residents should be able to ask before money moves." />

      <WhyThisMatters
        items={[
          "City spending decisions affect services, infrastructure, fees, reserves, and long-term obligations.",
          "Residents should be able to understand where money is building up, where projects are delayed, and what public benefit is expected.",
          "A living tracker helps connect budget numbers to real-world issues like sewer infrastructure, fire station repairs, street sweeping, grants, and contracts."
        ]}
      />

      <section class="explainer-box">
        <p class="explainer-kicker">Budget Watch Note</p>
        <h2>Why I asked about the sewer charge</h2>
        <p>
          At the July 7 West Covina City Council meeting, I asked a question about the Citywide Sewer Service Charge. This was not because sewer reserves are automatically bad. Sewer systems are expensive, and cities do need money set aside for maintenance, emergencies, repairs, and future infrastructure work.
        </p>
        <p>
          The reason I asked is because the staff report showed the sewer fund projected near $21 million, while also showing no major sewer capital improvement projects listed for FY 2026-27.
        </p>
        <p>
          That raises a fair resident question: if the City is continuing to collect the sewer charge, what is the repair plan tied to that money?
        </p>
        <p>
          The question is not simply why the City has reserves. The better question is what reserve level the City is targeting, what projects are being planned, and when residents will see a public sewer repair or upgrade schedule.
        </p>
        <p>
          StayJustIn submitted the question to the City and will update this tracker if a response is provided.
        </p>
      </section>

      <WhatWeKnow
        items={[
          "StayJustIn submitted written questions to the City on July 7, 2026 regarding the Citywide Sewer Service Charge and the sewer fund reserve.",
          "The question currently being tracked is why the sewer fund is projected near $21 million while no major sewer capital improvement projects were listed for FY 2026-27.",
          "This page will also track related public spending questions involving emergency repairs, budget amendments, grants, contracts, assessment districts, and service costs.",
          "StayJustIn is treating this as an ongoing accountability file rather than a one-time article."
        ]}
      />

      <WhatWeDontKnow
        items={[
          "The City's target reserve level for the sewer fund has not yet been confirmed to StayJustIn.",
          "The public sewer repair or upgrade plan tied to the projected reserve has not yet been confirmed to StayJustIn.",
          "It is not yet clear when future sewer capital projects are expected or why none were listed for FY 2026-27.",
          "Related spending questions involving Fire Station 3, street sweeping implementation, grants, and contracts will be added as records are reviewed."
        ]}
      />

      <QuestionsResidentsShouldAsk
        questions={[
          "What is the City's target reserve level for the sewer fund?",
          "Why are no major sewer capital improvement projects listed for FY 2026-27 if the fund is projected near $21 million?",
          "Will the City provide residents with a sewer repair or upgrade plan showing future work?",
          "Which funds are restricted, which are flexible, and which can only be used for specific purposes?",
          "How are emergency repairs, grants, contracts, and assessment districts reported back to residents after approval?",
          "What public dashboard or recurring report could help residents track spending after Council votes?"
        ]}
      />

      <AccountabilityTracker
        title="Budget & Spending Follow-Up Tracker"
        items={[
          {
            question: "Has the City confirmed the target sewer fund reserve level?",
            status: "pending",
            note: "Question submitted July 7, 2026. Awaiting City response."
          },
          {
            question: "Has the City explained why no major sewer capital projects were listed for FY 2026-27?",
            status: "pending",
            note: "Question submitted July 7, 2026. Awaiting City response."
          },
          {
            question: "Has the City provided a public sewer repair or upgrade plan?",
            status: "pending",
            note: "Question submitted July 7, 2026. Awaiting City response."
          },
          {
            question: "Has the City connected major spending items to plain-English resident impact?",
            status: "pending",
            note: "Ongoing tracker item for budget amendments, contracts, grants, emergency spending, and assessment districts."
          }
        ]}
      />

      <RelatedCoverage
        title="Related Accountability Coverage"
        items={[
          {
            title: "Fire Station 3 Emergency Repairs Tracker",
            href: "/accountability/fire-station-3/",
            description: "Track emergency repair costs, spending to date, remaining work, and the condition required to end emergency action."
          },
          {
            title: "Street Sweeping Enforcement Tracker",
            href: "/accountability/street-sweeping/",
            description: "Track enforcement timing, corrected maps, signs, warning cycles, and resident concerns."
          },
          {
            title: "City Hall Watch",
            href: "/city-hall/",
            description: "Track public meetings, budget decisions, staff reports, contracts, and public follow-up questions."
          }
        ]}
      />
    </div>
  </section>
</MainLayout>

<style>
  .adopted-budget { margin: 2rem 0; padding: clamp(1.1rem, 3vw, 2rem); border: 1px solid #cbd5e1; border-left: 6px solid #0f766e; border-radius: 0.9rem; background: #f8fafc; color: #172033; }
  .adopted-budget h2 { font-size: clamp(1.55rem, 3vw, 2.15rem); line-height: 1.2; }
  .adopted-budget h3 { margin-top: 1.6rem; font-size: 1.3rem; }
  .adopted-budget p, .adopted-budget li { font-size: 1.06rem; line-height: 1.7; }
  .adopted-budget li { margin: .5rem 0; }
  .budget-stats { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(180px, 1fr)); gap: .8rem; margin: 1.5rem 0; }
  .budget-stats > div { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: .3rem; padding: 1rem; background: #fff; border: 1px solid #dbe4ea; border-radius: .6rem; }
  .budget-stats span { font-size: .9rem; color: #475569; }
  .budget-stats strong { font-size: 1.3rem; color: #0f172a; }
  .budget-bars { display: grid; gap: .8rem; margin: 1rem 0 1.5rem; }
  .budget-bar-row { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(100px, 150px) minmax(50px, 1fr) 75px; gap: .65rem; align-items: center; font-size: .92rem; }
  .budget-bar-row strong { text-align: right; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  .budget-bar-track { background: #dbe4ea; height: 15px; border-radius: 5px; overflow: hidden; }
  .budget-bar-fill { height: 100%; background: #0f766e; border-radius: 5px; }
  .source-note { border-top: 1px solid #cbd5e1; padding-top: 1rem; font-size: .96rem !important; }
  @media (max-width: 480px) { .budget-bar-row { grid-template-columns: 95px minmax(35px, 1fr) 65px; gap: .4rem; font-size: .8rem; } }
  .explainer-box {
    margin: 2rem 0;
    padding: 1.5rem;
    border: 1px solid #e5e7eb;
    border-left: 5px solid #111827;
    border-radius: 0.85rem;
    background: #f8fafc;
  }

  .explainer-kicker {
    margin: 0 0 0.4rem;
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: #4b5563;
  }

  .explainer-box h2 {
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
  }

  .explainer-box p {
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .explainer-box p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
</style>
